WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
We want to describe a row
whose positions form a cycle.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:07.080
If an entry moves past the last
position, it should return to the first.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.180
Ordinary polynomials do not do
that automatically, so let us

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.080
change the rules to match the circle.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.500
Take the cyclic group generated by X,
with the rule X to the n equals one.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.700
Its elements are one, X, X squared,
through X to the n minus one.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.600
X is a formal marker for
one step around the cycle,

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.800
rather than a number we are
substituting. The integer

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.880
group ring attaches an integer coefficient
to each position and adds the terms.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.460
We add coefficients as usual, and multiply
with the rule X to the n equals one.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.940
This is the quotient of integer
polynomials by X to the n minus one.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:50.340
In the quotient, that
defining polynomial is zero.

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:56.020
For n equal to four, X to the
fourth is one, X to the fifth is X,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.600
and X inverse is X cubed.
For example, three X to

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.860
the fifth becomes three X: the exponent
wraps once around the four positions.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.060
A negative exponent moves
backward around the same cycle.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.300
Every element has a representative
of degree less than n.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:20.220
Replace X to the n by one, X to the n plus
one by X, and keep reducing exponents.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.780
Collect the terms whose exponents
land in the same position.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.380
The reduced representative is also unique.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.440
If two polynomials of degree less
than n represent the same element,

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:36.320
their difference is a multiple
of X to the n minus one. But any

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:41.400
nonzero such multiple has degree at least
n. The difference must therefore be zero.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:46.100
Thus the ordered coefficient list
is exactly the same information

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.680
as one group ring element
with degree less than n.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.780
Its positions are unambiguous. Next,
we will see why multiplying this

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:58.840
polynomial by X is precisely a
right cyclic shift of the row.
